\chapter{Functions as values}
A function expression uses \api{fn}, a parenthesized parameter list, and a
braced body. Bind it with \api{val} to give it a stable name, pass it to another
function, or store it in a collection. Parameters are local mutable bindings.

\begin{mullcode}
val twice <- fn(f, x){ return f(f(x)); };
val increment <- fn(x){ return x + 1; };
G:Assert(twice(increment, 10) == 12);
val adjust <- fn(x){ x <- x + 5; return x; };
var input <- 2;
G:Assert(adjust(input) == 7);
G:Assert(input == 2);
\end{mullcode}

\section{Arguments and results}
Arguments are evaluated left to right before the call begins. A script function
requires exactly its declared number of arguments. Duplicate parameter names
are rejected during parsing. There are no default arguments, rest parameters,
or overload resolution in the core language.

\api{return expression;} returns a value immediately. \api{return;} returns
nil. If control reaches the end of the body, the body returns the last
statement's value; an empty body returns nil. Explicit returns are usually
clearer in library interfaces, especially when the last statement is a loop
or a declaration.

Native functions use the same callable value kind. Their wrappers decide
whether an argument count is fixed or variadic and should report invalid
arguments with a MuLL error. The formatting library demonstrates both kinds:
\api{SetMainSink} takes one argument, while \api{Println} accepts any number.
